      "text": "In scientific literature, **maltose hydrolysis** is described as a terminal stage of carbohydrate digestion that occurs primarily on the surface of the **small intestinal epithelium**. While the **duodenum** prepares the chyme, the **jejunum** is the primary site where the hydrolysis of maltose into glucose molecules takes place.\n\nBelow are the key scientific publication sources and details regarding each part of the small intestine and its role:\n\n### 1. The Intestinal Epithelium (The \"Brush Border\")\nThe term \"epilethum\" in your request refers to the **epithelium**, specifically the simple columnar epithelium of the small intestine. Maltose hydrolysis does not occur freely in the lumen; instead, it happens at the **brush border** (microvilli) of these epithelial cells (enterocytes).\n*   **Source:** *Levitt, M. D., et al. (1996). \"Use of maltose hydrolysis measurements to characterize the interaction between the aqueous diffusion barrier and the epithelium in the rat jejunum.\" Journal of Clinical Investigation.*\n*   **Key Finding:** This study confirms that maltase activity is distributed over the intestinal villi. The enzymes responsible—**maltase-glucoamylase (MGAM)** and **sucrase-isomaltase (SI)**—are integral membrane proteins anchored in the brush border.\n\n### 2. The Jejunum (Primary Site of Hydrolysis)\nThe jejunum is the middle section of the small intestine and has the highest concentration of disaccharidase enzymes (like maltase) and the greatest surface area for absorption.\n*   **Source:** *Sandle, G. I., Lobley, R. W., \u0026 Holmes, R. (1982). \"Maltose Hydrolysis and Absorption in the Human Jejunum.\" Digestion.*\n*   **Key Finding:** This publication provides evidence that maltose hydrolysis in the human jejunum is a highly efficient process. It demonstrates that glucose released from maltose at the brush border is absorbed faster than free glucose, a phenomenon often called the \"kinetic advantage\" of disaccharide hydrolysis.\n\n### 3. The Duodenum (The Preparatory Stage)\nThe duodenum is the first part of the small intestine. While it contains brush border enzymes, its primary role in maltose digestion is the secretion of **pancreatic alpha-amylase**, which breaks down larger starches into maltose.\n*   **Source:** *Kushwaha, R., \u0026 Kumar, S. (2017). \"Small Intestine: Digestion and Absorption.\" In: Physiology of the Gastrointestinal Tract.*\n*   **Key Finding:** The duodenum serves as the mixing bowl where stomach chyme meets pancreatic enzymes. Although maltose hydrolysis begins here, the transit time is short, and the process reaches its peak in the proximal jejunum.\n\n### Summary Table of Roles\n| Part | Scientific Role in Maltose Digestion |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Duodenum** | Initial site of starch-to-maltose conversion via pancreatic amylase; beginning of mucosal hydrolysis. |\n| **Jejunum** | Major site for terminal maltose hydrolysis and subsequent glucose absorption. |\n| **Epithelium** | The cellular layer containing **maltase** and **sucrase-isomaltase** enzymes embedded in the microvilli (brush border). |\n\n### Recommended Textbook Reference\nFor a comprehensive overview that cites these processes, you may also refer to:\n*   **Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology**: Chapter on \"Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract\" (specifically the section on Carbohydrate Digestion).",
