        "text": "In scientific literature, **maltose hydrolysis** is described as a terminal stage of carbohydrate digestion that occurs primarily on the surface of the **small intestinal epithelium**. While the **duodenum** prepares"
      }, {
        "text": " the chyme, the **jejunum** is the primary site where the hydrolysis of maltose into glucose molecules takes place.\n\n"
      }, {
        "text": "Below are the key scientific publication sources and details regarding each part of the small intestine and its role:\n\n### 1"
      }, {
        "text": ". The Intestinal Epithelium (The \"Brush Border\")\nThe term \"epilethum\" in your request"
      }, {
        "text": " refers to the **epithelium**, specifically the simple columnar epithelium of the small intestine. Maltose hydrolysis does not occur freely"
      }, {
        "text": " in the lumen; instead, it happens at the **brush border** (microvilli) of these epithelial cells ("
      }, {
        "text": "enterocytes).\n*   **Source:** *Levitt, M. D., et al. (1996)."
      }, {
        "text": " \"Use of maltose hydrolysis measurements to characterize the interaction between the aqueous diffusion barrier and the epithelium in the rat jejunum"
      }, {
        "text": ".\" Journal of Clinical Investigation.*\n*   **Key Finding:** This study confirms that maltase activity is distributed over the intestinal villi"
      }, {
        "text": ". The enzymes responsible—**maltase-glucoamylase (MGAM)** and **sucrase-isomaltase"
      }, {
        "text": " (SI)**—are integral membrane proteins anchored in the brush border.\n\n### 2. The Jejunum (Primary Site"
      }, {
        "text": " of Hydrolysis)\nThe jejunum is the middle section of the small intestine and has the highest concentration of disaccharidase enzymes ("
      }, {
        "text": "like maltase) and the greatest surface area for absorption.\n*   **Source:** *Sandle, G. I"
      }, {
        "text": "., Lobley, R. W., \u0026 Holmes, R. (1982). \"Maltose Hydrolysis and"
      }, {
        "text": " Absorption in the Human Jejunum.\" Digestion.*\n*   **Key Finding:** This publication provides evidence that maltose"
      }, {
        "text": " hydrolysis in the human jejunum is a highly efficient process. It demonstrates that glucose released from maltose at the brush border is"
      }, {
        "text": " absorbed faster than free glucose, a phenomenon often called the \"kinetic advantage\" of disaccharide hydrolysis.\n\n### 3. The"
      }, {
        "text": " Duodenum (The Preparatory Stage)\nThe duodenum is the first part of the small intestine. While it contains brush"
      }, {
        "text": " border enzymes, its primary role in maltose digestion is the secretion of **pancreatic alpha-amylase**, which breaks"
      }, {
        "text": " down larger starches into maltose.\n*   **Source:** *Kushwaha, R., \u0026 Kumar, S"
      }, {
        "text": ". (2017). \"Small Intestine: Digestion and Absorption.\" In: Physiology of the Gastrointestinal Tract.*"
      }, {
        "text": "\n*   **Key Finding:** The duodenum serves as the mixing bowl where stomach chyme meets pancreatic enzymes. Although malt"
      }, {
        "text": "ose hydrolysis begins here, the transit time is short, and the process reaches its peak in the proximal jejunum.\n\n###"
      }, {
        "text": " Summary Table of Roles\n| Part | Scientific Role in Maltose Digestion |\n| :--- | :--- |\n|"
      }, {
        "text": " **Duodenum** | Initial site of starch-to-maltose conversion via pancreatic amylase; beginning of mucosal hydrolysis"
      }, {
        "text": ". |\n| **Jejunum** | Major site for terminal maltose hydrolysis and subsequent glucose absorption. |\n| **E"
      }, {
        "text": "pithelium** | The cellular layer containing **maltase** and **sucrase-isomaltase** enzymes embedded in the micro"
      }, {
        "text": "villi (brush border). |\n\n### Recommended Textbook Reference\nFor a comprehensive overview that cites these processes, you may"
      }, {
        "text": " also refer to:\n*   **Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology**: Chapter on \"Digestion and Absorption in"
      }, {
        "text": " the Gastrointestinal Tract\" (specifically the section on Carbohydrate Digestion)."
