      "text": "The primary site of maltose hydrolysis in the human body is the **jejunum**, specifically at the **brush border (microvilli) of the intestinal epithelium**.\n\nWhile the duodenum begins the process of carbohydrate digestion, the majority of terminal hydrolysis—the final step of breaking down maltose into two glucose molecules—occurs in the jejunum.\n\n### 1. Direct Publication Source\nThe most direct scientific publication confirming this is:\n*   **Source:** *Sandle, G. I., Lobley, R. W., \u0026 Holmes, R. (1982). \"Maltose Hydrolysis and Absorption in the Human Jejunum.\" Digestion, 24(4), 245–251.*\n*   **Direct Finding:** This study used intestinal perfusion to demonstrate that maltose is hydrolyzed rapidly and efficiently in the **human jejunum**. It highlights that the \"kinetic advantage\" (faster absorption of glucose from maltose than from free glucose) is a hallmark of the jejunal brush border\u0027s enzymatic efficiency.\n\n### 2. Supporting Textbook Evidence\nIf you require a broader academic reference used in medical education:\n*   **Source:** *Hall, J. E., \u0026 Hall, M. E. (2020). Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology (14th Ed.). Elsevier.*\n*   **Context:** In the chapter \"Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract,\" the text specifies that the terminal digestion of disaccharides (like maltose) occurs when they come into contact with the enterocytes of the **proximal jejunum**. It notes that the enzymes **maltase** and **α-dextrinase** are embedded in the brush border of these cells to complete the hydrolysis.\n\n### Summary of Locations\n| Part | Scientific Role | Status as \"Primary Site\" |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Duodenum** | Receives pancreatic amylase; initial starch-to-maltose conversion. | **Secondary** (mostly preparatory). |\n| **Jejunum** | Contains the highest concentration of maltase enzymes and the largest surface area. | **Primary Site** (terminal hydrolysis). |\n| **Epithelium** | The specific cellular layer (enterocytes) where enzymes are anchored. | **Microscopic Site** (mechanism). |\n\n**Conclusion:** If you are citing a location for a paper or exam, the **jejunum** is the correct anatomical answer, and the **brush border of the epithelium** is the correct physiological/cellular answer.",
