        "text": "The primary site of maltose hydrolysis in the human body is the **jejunum**, specifically at the **brush border (microvilli) of the intestinal epithelium**.\n\nWhile the duodenum begins"
      }, {
        "text": " the process of carbohydrate digestion, the majority of terminal hydrolysis—the final step of breaking down maltose into two glucose molecules—oc"
      }, {
        "text": "curs in the jejunum.\n\n### 1. Direct Publication Source\nThe most direct scientific publication confirming this is:"
      }, {
        "text": "\n*   **Source:** *Sandle, G. I., Lobley, R. W., \u0026 Holmes, R"
      }, {
        "text": ". (1982). \"Maltose Hydrolysis and Absorption in the Human Jejunum.\" Digestion,"
      }, {
        "text": " 24(4), 245–251.*\n*   **Direct Finding:** This study used"
      }, {
        "text": " intestinal perfusion to demonstrate that maltose is hydrolyzed rapidly and efficiently in the **human jejunum**. It highlights that the \"kinetic advantage\""
      }, {
        "text": " (faster absorption of glucose from maltose than from free glucose) is a hallmark of the jejunal brush border\u0027s enzymatic efficiency"
      }, {
        "text": ".\n\n### 2. Supporting Textbook Evidence\nIf you require a broader academic reference used in medical education:\n*   **"
      }, {
        "text": "Source:** *Hall, J. E., \u0026 Hall, M. E. (2020). Guyton"
      }, {
        "text": " and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology (14th Ed.). Elsevier.*\n*   **Context:** In the chapter \""
      }, {
        "text": "Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract,\" the text specifies that the terminal digestion of disaccharides (like maltose) occurs"
      }, {
        "text": " when they come into contact with the enterocytes of the **proximal jejunum**. It notes that the enzymes **maltase** and"
      }, {
        "text": " **α-dextrinase** are embedded in the brush border of these cells to complete the hydrolysis.\n\n###"
      }, {
        "text": " Summary of Locations\n| Part | Scientific Role | Status as \"Primary Site\" |\n| :--- | :---"
      }, {
        "text": " | :--- |\n| **Duodenum** | Receives pancreatic amylase; initial starch-to-maltose conversion."
      }, {
        "text": " | **Secondary** (mostly preparatory). |\n| **Jejunum** | Contains the highest concentration of maltase enzymes and"
      }, {
        "text": " the largest surface area. | **Primary Site** (terminal hydrolysis). |\n| **Epithelium** | The specific"
      }, {
        "text": " cellular layer (enterocytes) where enzymes are anchored. | **Microscopic Site** (mechanism). |\n\n**Conclusion"
      }, {
        "text": ":** If you are citing a location for a paper or exam, the **jejunum** is the correct anatomical answer, and the"
      }, {
        "text": " **brush border of the epithelium** is the correct physiological/cellular answer."
