        "text": "In scientific research, the \"lumen volume\" of the human small intestine is measured in two ways: as the **anatomical capacity** (the theoretical maximum volume) and as the **functional fluid volume** (the actual amount of liquid"
      }, {
        "text": " present, which is critical for hydrolysis and drug dissolution).\n\nThe most precise current sources for these values are derived from **Magnetic Resonance"
      }, {
        "text": " Imaging (MRI)** studies.\n\n### 1. Functional Fluid Volume (Small Bowel Water Content)\nThis is the"
      }, {
        "text": " most common value used in scientific publications regarding digestion and pharmacology. It represents the actual volume available for maltose hydrolysis.\n\n*   **"
      }, {
        "text": "Fasted State:** **43 ± 14 mL**  \n    *   **Source:** *Mudie, D"
      }, {
        "text": ". M., et al. (2014). \"Quantification of Gastrointestinal Liquid Volumes and Distribution Following a"
      }, {
        "text": " 240 mL Dose of Water in the Fasted State.\" Molecular Pharmaceutics, 11(9"
      }, {
        "text": "), 3036–3047.*\n    *   **Context:** This study used MRI to show"
      }, {
        "text": " that the small intestine does not contain a continuous column of liquid but rather small \"pockets.\"\n*   **Alternative"
      }, {
        "text": " Fasted/Fed Values:** **105 mL (Fasted) vs. 54 mL (Fed)**"
      }, {
        "text": "\n    *   **Source:** *Schiller, C., et al. (2005). \"Intestinal"
      }, {
        "text": " fluid volumes and transit of dosage forms as assessed by magnetic resonance imaging.\" Alimentary Pharmacology \u0026 Therapeutics, 22(10"
      }, {
        "text": "), 971–979.*\n    *   **Finding:** Interestingly, this study found that the"
      }, {
        "text": " total volume of free liquid in the small intestine actually *decreases* shortly after a meal (due to rapid absorption and"
      }, {
        "text": " transit), despite the intake of food.\n\n### 2. Total Luminal Volume (Including Gas and Content)\nIf"
      }, {
        "text": " you are looking for the total space occupied by fluid, gas, and food residue:\n*   **Typical Volume:** **~7"
      }, {
        "text": "13 mL**\n    *   **Source:** *Frokjaer, J. B., et al. (20"
      }, {
        "text": "07). \"Magnetic resonance imaging of the small intestine in healthy subjects and patients with irritable bowel syndrome.\" (Later cited"
      }, {
        "text": " in studies like \"Gastric Emptying Time and Volume of the Small Intestine,\" 2021).*\n    *   "
      }, {
        "text": "**Details:** In healthy controls, the total volume of the small intestinal lumen (liquid + gas) is approximately 70"
      }, {
        "text": "0–900 cm³ (mL).\n\n### 3. Anatomical Capacity (Theoretical)\nThis is"
      }, {
        "text": " the volume calculated based on the organ\u0027s physical dimensions. \n*   **Dimensions:** Length of ~6–7"
      }, {
        "text": " meters and a diameter of ~2.5–3 cm.\n*   **Calculated Volume:** Approximately **3,"
      }, {
        "text": "000–3,500 mL (3–3.5 Liters)**.\n*   **Reference"
      }, {
        "text": ":** *Standring, S. (2020). Gray\u0027s Anatomy: The Anatomical Basis of Clinical Practice.*\n\n"
      }, {
        "text": "### Summary Table for Citation\n| State | Volume Type | Typical Value | Primary Source |\n| :--- | :"
      }, {
        "text": "--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Fasted** | Free Fluid (Water) | **43"
      }, {
        "text": " mL** | Mudie et al. (2014) |\n| **Fasted** | Total Lum"
      }, {
        "text": "inal (Fluid + Gas) | **713 mL** | Frokjaer et al. (2007"
      }, {
        "text": ") |\n| **Fed** | Free Fluid (Water) | **54 mL** | Schiller et al."
      }, {
        "text": " (2005) |\n\n**Recommendation:** If you are citing this in the context of **maltose hydrolysis**,"
      }, {
        "text": " use the **Mudie et al. (2014)** or **Schiller et al. (200"
      }, {
        "text": "5)** sources, as they describe the \"aqueous environment\" where the enzymes in the epithelium interact with the maltose."
